When Andrej Karpathy posted about building an =E2=80=9CLLM Wiki [ https://s=
ubstack.com/redirect/23b90808-2f4b-4860-bd57-a2c09b1743ab?j=3DeyJ1IjoiNHFmM=
XoifQ.Sp_5ata0UMGzgUB5_ABJSdjpWW-60hATxZ3txbIxoTo ]=E2=80=9D in April and g=
arnered 19.6 million views, he started a trend: an army of geeks building t=
heir own =E2=80=9CAI brains.=E2=80=9D=20
I=E2=80=99m one of them. So is Brian Halligan, the former CEO of HubSpot an=
d now partner at Sequoia, who revealed he=E2=80=99d built an AI agent train=
ed on his entire life that now attends his Zoom meetings. He calls it =E2=
=80=98Hal [ https://substack.com/redirect/7789da89-960b-4f54-970d-b8b37438f=
28c?j=3DeyJ1IjoiNHFmMXoifQ.Sp_5ata0UMGzgUB5_ABJSdjpWW-60hATxZ3txbIxoTo ]=E2=
=80=99. Tiago Forte, the person who coined =E2=80=9CBuilding a Second Brain=
 [ https://substack.com/redirect/4ed3229f-5d90-48a9-882b-9f7beb1a41af?j=3De=
yJ1IjoiNHFmMXoifQ.Sp_5ata0UMGzgUB5_ABJSdjpWW-60hATxZ3txbIxoTo ],=E2=80=9D t=
ook his original system, which relied on you doing all the organizing, and =
handed that job to AI agents. Salesforce=E2=80=99s President of Enterprise =
AI revealed he dumps stream-of-consciousness thoughts into a private Slack =
channel so AI can reason across months of his thinking. Jason Lemkin built =
an AI version of himself at SaaStr that his team can talk to any time, trai=
ned on decades of SaaS knowledge. And, of course, I have also gone all in o=
n my =E2=80=9CAI brain [ https://substack.com/redirect/6861b776-11c6-46b8-a=
e17-7d4c4ea69cc0?j=3DeyJ1IjoiNHFmMXoifQ.Sp_5ata0UMGzgUB5_ABJSdjpWW-60hATxZ3=
txbIxoTo ]=E2=80=9D.
Something is happening. The smartest people in tech are racing to build per=
sonal AI systems that know everything they know, remember everything they f=
orget, and, in some cases, act on their behalf. They=E2=80=99re calling the=
m =E2=80=9CAI Second Brains,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=9CAI Agents,=E2=80=9D =E2=80=
=9CDigital Twins.=E2=80=9D What are they? They give AI the full context of =
who you are, how you think, and what you=E2=80=99ve learned, then let it co=
mpound that knowledge over time.
For those following me, you know I=E2=80=99ve been building my own for the =
past three months using Claude Code, Obsidian and Git. It=E2=80=99s made me=
 a dramatically better leader, with faster and higher-quality decisions on =
things that used to take me days to think through.
This post covers why people are building these systems, where the concept i=
s heading, and five examples from people who are actually doing it.
Why Now?
The idea of a =E2=80=9Csecond brain=E2=80=9D isn=E2=80=99t new. Tiago Forte=
 popularised the concept with his book Building a Second Brain, which sold =
over 500,000 copies and taught people a methodology for capturing, organisi=
ng, and retrieving their notes and ideas. Before that, people tried wikis, =
Evernote, Notion databases, Zettelkasten systems, and a dozen other approac=
hes to managing what they know.
They all had the same fatal flaw: you had to maintain them. And nobody does=
=2E Not really. Not for long enough=
 for it to matter. Everyone=E2=80=99s =E2=
=80=98file=E2=80=99 system will eventually decay.=20
In the 1980s, Ray Dalio started writing down every decision he made at Brid=
gewater Associates and the reasoning behind it. Over decades, this became h=
is =E2=80=9CPrinciples=E2=80=9D system: a codified repository of how he thi=
nks, what he values, and what he=E2=80=99s learned from every mistake. New =
employees studied it. Decisions were cross-referenced against it. It became=
 Bridgewater=E2=80=99s institutional memory.
It worked. It also required decades of manual effort and a team to maintain=
=2E Dalio could sustain it because he r=
an a $150 billion fund and had the dis=
cipline and resources to keep the system alive.
What=E2=80=99s changed is that AI removes the maintenance. The AI reads, wr=
ites, organizes, links, and retrieves. You just talk to it. Context windows=
 got big enough, agents got capable enough, and tools like Claude Code made=
 it possible to point an LLM at a folder and ask it to enrich the folder wi=
th data from across a wide spectrum of sources.
But it=E2=80=99s not just about remembering things. The real pull is compou=
nding. The more you feed the system, the smarter it gets. Connections form =
that you=E2=80=99d never draw yourself. An idea from February resurfaces in=
 June because the AI spotted the link. People who build these systems all d=
escribe the same feeling: it=E2=80=99s like having a version of yourself th=
at never forgets. Even better, it=E2=80=99s a place to keep all the intelli=
gence you gain through years of work. What worked, what didn=E2=80=99t, why=
 certain decisions were made, why that big bet turned out to be so successf=
ul.
As Joe Inzerillo, Salesforce=E2=80=99s President of Enterprise and AI Techn=
ology, put it: =E2=80=9CHuman intellect is amazing, but our memory is very =
flawed. Reasoning across huge volumes of data is something LLMs just don=E2=
=80=99t break a sweat with.=E2=80=9D
The Architecture Question Nobody Has Solved Yet
Here=E2=80=99s the honest tension: if you have a personal brain, a team bra=
in, and a company brain, how do they actually connect? This is one of the h=
arder problems to solve.
Today, I have a =E2=80=98work brain=E2=80=99 for HubSpot and a =E2=80=98per=
sonal brain=E2=80=99 for Kieran. It means I have to switch between two brai=
ns; they don=E2=80=99t share any knowledge; it=E2=80=99s inefficient. I=E2=
=80=99m in the process of building our first team brain at HubSpot, and the=
 company brain at the moment is in Glean. The question is how you bring all=
 these together in a much smarter way.
The emerging pattern looks like layers with permissions rather than three s=
eparate systems. And for good reason. If you split into three separate brai=
ns, you lose the compounding benefit. A personal insight that=E2=80=99s use=
ful for the team never surfaces. A company decision that should change your=
 personal priorities stays siloed.
Think of when I=E2=80=99m doing advisory or helping a founder. I=E2=80=99ll=
 start the call by connecting Claude Code or Codex to my brain, so instead =
of having to remember every relevant piece of work I=E2=80=99ve done to bri=
ng up during the conversation, my brain can do it. But maybe there is somet=
hing we learned in HubSpot, relevant to our conversation, and I can package=
 up in a best-practice way without disclosing any proprietary info; I=E2=80=
=99d want to do that. Today, it=E2=80=99s hard; I=E2=80=99d need to switch =
between brains.
The model forming is a knowledge layer with scopes. Your personal context i=
s private by default. Team context is shared within your team. Company cont=
ext is available to everyone. You choose what crosses boundaries.
The analogy is how we already handle documents. You have private files, sha=
red team drives, and company-wide resources. Same data architecture, applie=
d to AI memory.
But there=E2=80=99s a real constraint. Your company=E2=80=99s proprietary c=
ustomer data shouldn=E2=80=99t live in the same unscoped system as your per=
sonal career notes. Regulated industries have compliance requirements. Even=
 in a startup, there are things the CEO=E2=80=99s second brain knows that s=
houldn=E2=80=99t flow freely to every team member=E2=80=99s agent.
Nobody has fully solved this. But I think it=E2=80=99s a fascinating proble=
m to consider. Today, I=E2=80=99d mostly focus on how you can build a solo =
brain that can ingest all your intelligence in a smart way, continually enr=
ich itself, and be plug-and-play with your AI model of choice.
Five People Building Their AI Second Brain Right Now
This isn=E2=80=99t theoretical. Here are five examples of well-known tech t=
hought leaders building these systems today, each with a different approach=
=2E I=E2=80=99ve ordered them from the mos=
t passive (a knowledge system the A=
I maintains) to the most active (a digital twin that acts on your behalf).
Andrej Karpathy =E2=80=94 The LLM Wiki
Former Director of AI at Tesla and co-founder of OpenAI. One of the most re=
spected AI researchers alive.
Karpathy built what he calls an =E2=80=9CLLM Wiki.=E2=80=9D You drop raw so=
urces (articles, papers, book notes, podcast takeaways) into a folder. The =
LLM reads everything, writes summary pages, creates entity and concept page=
s, cross-references everything, and resolves contradictions. His vault reac=
hed roughly 100 articles and 400,000 words, all written and maintained by t=
he agent. He rarely edits the wiki manually. That=E2=80=99s the domain of t=
he LLM.
His framing is the cleanest articulation of the concept I=E2=80=99ve seen: =
=E2=80=9CObsidian is the IDE. The LLM is the programmer. The wiki is the co=
debase.=E2=80=9D
This was the post that kicked off the AI brain craze.
Source: X post, April 2026 [ https://substack.com/redirect/23b90808-2f4b-48=
60-bd57-a2c09b1743ab?j=3DeyJ1IjoiNHFmMXoifQ.Sp_5ata0UMGzgUB5_ABJSdjpWW-60hA=
TxZ3txbIxoTo ] (19.6M views, 55K likes)
Tiago Forte =E2=80=94 The AI Second Brain
Author of Building a Second Brain (500,000 copies sold), creator of the PAR=
A method. The person who coined the =E2=80=9Csecond brain=E2=80=9D concept =
for knowledge work.
Forte rebuilt the entire concept for AI. His system centres on what he call=
s Personal Context Management: a Master Prompt that gives AI your full pers=
onal context, an AI-adapted PARA structure that feeds the right information=
 on demand, an =E2=80=9CAI Board of Advisors=E2=80=9D (multiple agent persp=
ectives for decision-making), and long-term memory with sub-agents.
His thesis is: =E2=80=9CThe bottleneck isn=E2=80=99t the AI model. It=E2=80=
=99s what the model knows about you before you ask.=E2=80=9D
Source: Forte Labs blog [ https://substack.com/redirect/358e2cb2-c2f5-44fd-=
9cb7-dbf8e9a57ae5?j=3DeyJ1IjoiNHFmMXoifQ.Sp_5ata0UMGzgUB5_ABJSdjpWW-60hATxZ=
3txbIxoTo ] + cohort program, April-May 2026 [ https://substack.com/redirec=
t/a95de85a-aca4-4ba3-803d-9ec087fc9162?j=3DeyJ1IjoiNHFmMXoifQ.Sp_5ata0UMGzg=
UB5_ABJSdjpWW-60hATxZ3txbIxoTo ]
Joe Inzerillo =E2=80=94 The Salesforce Second Brain
President of Enterprise & AI Technology at Salesforce, leading Agentforce, =
Slack, and the company=E2=80=99s =E2=80=9CCustomer Zero=E2=80=9D strategy.
Inzerillo=E2=80=99s approach is surprisingly simple for someone running AI =
at a Fortune 500 company. He uses a private Slack channel as a stream-of-co=
nsciousness feed: wakes up at 2am with an idea, dumps it into the channel, =
and Slackbot synthesises it with months of prior context. He connected his =
personal email to Claude. No Obsidian vault, no custom code. Just a Slack c=
hannel and an LLM that reasons across everything he=E2=80=99s ever put into=
 it.
At Salesforce, the institutional version is running too. His team compresse=
d a 231-day migration into 13 days using AI agents. His recommended first s=
tep for anyone: =E2=80=9CConnect your email to an LLM. Do it in a safe way.=
 Just go to town.=E2=80=9D
Source: YouTube interview with Allie K Miller, June 2026 [ https://substack=
=2Ecom/redirect/63556232-a2e7-4f6d-88d=
3-040ef9fd718d?j=3DeyJ1IjoiNHFmMXoifQ.S=
p_5ata0UMGzgUB5_ABJSdjpWW-60hATxZ3txbIxoTo ]
Jason Lemkin =E2=80=94 The SaaStr AI Mentor
Founder of SaaStr, one of the largest SaaS communities in the world.
Lemkin took the concept from personal to institutional. He built an AI vari=
ant at SaaStr that his team can talk to any time. It stores daily summaries=
 to memory, creating a compounding knowledge layer. Think of it as decades =
of SaaS knowledge made queryable for his entire team and community, not jus=
t the things Lemkin remembers to mention.
His version sits in the =E2=80=9Cteam brain=E2=80=9D category: institutiona=
l knowledge that doesn=E2=80=99t depend on one person=E2=80=99s memory or a=
vailability.
Source: X reply to Halligan, June 2026 [ https://substack.com/redirect/d95a=
c2cc-2965-45bc-a642-7a215684f233?j=3DeyJ1IjoiNHFmMXoifQ.Sp_5ata0UMGzgUB5_AB=
JSdjpWW-60hATxZ3txbIxoTo ] + saastr.ai/ai-mentor [ https://substack.com/red=
irect/a4c239af-6b73-41a7-b28e-a35b75855692?j=3DeyJ1IjoiNHFmMXoifQ.Sp_5ata0U=
MGzgUB5_ABJSdjpWW-60hATxZ3txbIxoTo ]
Brian Halligan =E2=80=94 =E2=80=9CHal,=E2=80=9D The Digital Twin
Co-founder and former CEO of HubSpot.
Halligan represents the furthest end of the spectrum. He built an AI agent =
named =E2=80=9CHal,=E2=80=9D trained on his entire life. Hal runs his day, =
sends emails, organizes projects, collaborates with his team, and now parti=
cipates in his Zoom meetings. This isn=E2=80=99t a knowledge wiki. It=E2=80=
=99s a digital twin that acts as him.
=E2=80=9CHe=E2=80=99s not just a copilot anymore. He is me.=E2=80=9D
This is Brian=E2=80=99s setup (it=E2=80=99s dope and has given me a tonne o=
f inspiration for V2 of mine.
Halligan=E2=80=99s next step: Hal handling meetings and working independent=
ly. Moving from copilot to full autopilot.
Source: X post, June 2026 [ https://substack.com/redirect/7789da89-960b-4f5=
4-970d-b8b37438f28c?j=3DeyJ1IjoiNHFmMXoifQ.Sp_5ata0UMGzgUB5_ABJSdjpWW-60hAT=
xZ3txbIxoTo ]
And, a little plug for my upcoming V2 to be published here first :)
Kieran Flanagan =E2=80=94 The Marketing Leader=E2=80=99s AI Brain
SVP of Agentic GTM and Systems at HubSpot. (but really still a marketer and=
 growth person lol)
I=E2=80=99ve been building my own AI second brain for the past three months=
 using Claude Code, Obsidian and Git. One is a system of MCP tools that man=
ages my content strategy, researches topics, drafts posts, tracks what=E2=
=80=99s working, and remembers every decision I=E2=80=99ve made about my au=
dience, my voice, and my priorities. It runs on markdown files, custom skil=
ls I=E2=80=99ve built over time, and a growing vault of context that makes =
every conversation with AI dramatically more useful than the last. The othe=
r is my =E2=80=9CHubSpot=E2=80=9D brain, that captures all relevant context=
 across my work, sets my priorities, helps solves hard problems, ensures I =
never miss a single piece of important data.=20
It=E2=80=99s made me a 10x better leader because it holds the full context =
of my work, so I never start from zero. Every session picks up where the la=
st one left off.
I=E2=80=99m currently working on v2 of both (potentially merging them).=20
Source: I Built an AI Second Brain [ https://substack.com/redirect/6861b776=
-11c6-46b8-ae17-7d4c4ea69cc0?j=3DeyJ1IjoiNHFmMXoifQ.Sp_5ata0UMGzgUB5_ABJSdj=
pWW-60hATxZ3txbIxoTo ]
Where is this going?
The argument is this might be a trend for AI geeks and developers only.=20
Look at the range of examples I gave. Brian Halligan isn=E2=80=99t a develo=
per. He=E2=80=99s an ex-CEO. Inzerillo uses a private Slack channel and con=
nected email. Forte built a system around master prompts and organised fold=
ers. The spectrum runs from =E2=80=9Cpoint Claude Code at a folder=E2=80=9D=
 to =E2=80=9Cjust connect your email to an LLM.=E2=80=9D The entry point is=
 lower than it looks from the outside. And the tools are moving toward the =
user, not the other way around. Claude=E2=80=99s Cowork mode, Notion AI, Sl=
ack=E2=80=99s native AI layer, connecting to Git: these are getting simpler=
 every quarter, not more complex.
The people building these systems now are creating an asset that compounds =
daily. Every conversation, every meeting, every decision feeds the system a=
nd makes it smarter. Everyone else is starting from zero every time they op=
en a new chat window.
The gap between those two groups widens every week.
Until Next Time,
Happy AI=E2=80=99fying,=20
Kieran

